  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nQuiet and low-impact weather will persist through Tuesday under\nthe influence of a mid-level ridge and building surface high \npressure over the Great Lakes region. Temperatures will remain \ntypical for late August, featuring afternoon highs in the low \n80s. Low relative humidity levels will allow overnight lows to \ndrop into the 50s, creating pleasant evening conditions.\n\nA pattern shift is expected by Wednesday as a well-defined \nshortwave trough and associated surface cold front track out of \nthe Canadian Prairies and across the Great Lakes. Southerly \nsurface winds ahead of the advancing frontal boundary will drive\nWednesday afternoon temperatures into the mid-80s. Moderate \ninstability is anticipated in the boundary layer, supported by a\nnarrow plume of low-70s dewpoints pooling ahead of the front \nthat will help yield MLCAPE values around 2500 J/kg. Overlapping\nwith approximately 30 knots of bulk shear, this buoyancy will \nfavor organized supercellular structures along and ahead of the \nfront. Forecast mesoscale soundings for Wednesday afternoon \nfeature straight or subtle veer-back-veer hodographs, indicating\nprimary threats of hail and damaging winds over a tornado risk.\nHigh-resolution CAM guidance shows mixed signals regarding \nconvective coverage. This uncertainty stems from the strength of\nsynoptic forcing over central Illinois, as the primary \nshortwave displacement well to the north may keep overall \nforcing and storm coverage limited.\n\nLimited moisture return ahead of Wednesday's front is keeping \nQPF down. With forecast PWATs around 1.5 inches, and progressive\natmospheric flow, the flash flood risk from convective precip \nis quite low.\n\nFollowing the passage of the front, dry and less humid \nconditions will take hold Thursday through Saturday as an \nelongated central U.S. upper ridge promotes the gradual build of\na continental polar (cP) airmass across the Midwest and Great \nLakes. Daytime highs will hover around 80F, with overnight lows\ndipping into the 50s for the remainder of the workweek.\n\nBy early next week, medium-range global ensembles indicate a \nnotable warming trend as a strong upper trough makes landfall in\nthe Pacific Northwest, driving the ridge axis eastward into the\nMidwest. The combined presence of a 588-594 mb ridge core and \nweak low-level return flow on its western flank will heighten \nthe potential for triple-digit heat indices early next week.",
